description:
The sku_add.php endpoint is vulnerable to Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks. The endpoint accepts POST requests without any CSRF token validation, session token verification, Referer/Origin header checking, or authorization validation. An attacker can craft a malicious HTML form on an external website that, when visited by an authenticated user, will automatically submit a POST request to create unauthorized SKU records without the user's knowledge or consent.

The vulnerable code shows:
```php
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
    $sku = $_POST['sku'];
    $name = $_POST['name'];
    $description = $_POST['description'];
    $db = include 'database.php';
    $stmt = $db->prepare('INSERT INTO skus (sku, name, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?)');
    $stmt->execute([$sku, $name, $description]);
    header('Location: sku_system.php');
    exit();
}
```

The template (sku_add.twig) contains a form without CSRF token protection:
```html
<form method="post" action="sku_add.php">
    <input type="text" name="sku" required>
    <input type="text" name="name" required>
    <textarea name="description"></textarea>
    <button type="submit">Add</button>
</form>
```

No CSRF token field is present in the form.

remediation:
1. Implement CSRF token generation and validation:
   ```php
   session_start();
   if (empty($_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {
       $_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));
   }

   if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {
       if (!hash_equals($_POST['csrf_token'] ?? '', $_SESSION['csrf_token'])) {
           http_response_code(403);
           exit('CSRF token validation failed');
       }
       // ... proceed with ADD logic
   }
   ```

2. Add CSRF token to the Twig template:
   ```html
   <form method="post" action="sku_add.php">
       <input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
       <input type="text" name="sku" required>
       <input type="text" name="name" required>
       <textarea name="description"></textarea>
       <button type="submit">Add</button>
   </form>
   ```

3. Implement SameSite cookie attribute on session cookies:
   ```php
   ini_set('session.cookie_samesite', 'Strict');
   ```

4. Implement Content-Security-Policy headers to restrict form submissions

5. Add server-side authorization checks to verify user has permission to add SKUs

6. Implement input validation and sanitization for all POST parameters

7. Consider implementing double-submit cookie pattern as additional defense

8. Log all SKU creation operations for audit purposes
